//what this code does//
It demonstrate the typical memory area..
        Process Memory
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ Code / Text             │ ← functions (add, main)
├─────────────────────────┤
│ Data                    │ ← global variable n
├─────────────────────────┤
│ Heap                    │ ← malloc()
│                         │
├─────────────────────────┤
│ Stack                   │ ← local variables x,y,p
└─────────────────────────┘

1.Global variable: n is declared outside all functions, so it is a global variable.
2.Functions → Code/Text area,At what memory address is the machine code for add() located?At what memory address is the machine code for main() located?
3.Local Variable: x,y,p local variables, so they normally reside on the stack.
4.malloc() → Heap ,bytes are requested from the heap.malloc() returns the address of that memory.

Difference between &p and p

This is probably the most important concept in this program.
Suppose:

p = 0x5000

Then:

&p

is the address of the pointer variable itself.
For example:

Stack

Address 0x7000
       ┌───────────┐
       │     p     │
       │  0x5000   │
       └───────────┘
           ↑
          &p

But p contains:

0x5000

which points to the heap:

Heap

0x5000
┌────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐
│    │    │    │    │ ...│
└────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘

Therefore:

&p

→ address of the pointer variable p (stack)

while:

p

→ address of the dynamically allocated memory (heap)
